#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" :question/math-academy-id "e-15071" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-4w^2-30w-50$." :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-4w^2-30w-50\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $-2$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2$:\n\n$$\n-2(2w^2+15w+25)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $2w^2+15w+25$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 25 = 50$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $50$.\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $50$ are\n\n$$\n(1,50),\\quad (2,25),\\quad (5,10)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,10)$ add to $15{:}$\n\n$$\n5+10 =15 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5w+10w$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2w^{2} + 15w + 25 &=  \\\\\n2w^{2} + 5w + 10w + 25 &=  \\\\\n2w^{2} + 10w + 5w + 25 &=  \\\\\n(2w^{2} + 10w) + (5w + 25) &=  \\\\\n2w(w + 5) + 5(w + 5) &=  \\\\\n&= (2w + 5)(w + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n-2(2w + 5)(w + 5)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203745 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15088" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20$." :question/worked-solution "We must factor the following expression:\n$-10t^2 - 35t +20$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5$:\n\n$$\n-5(2t^{2} + 7t - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n$(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4)$.\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$\n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t^{2} + 7t - 4 &=  \\\\\n2t^{2} - t + 8t - 4 &=  \\\\\n(2t^{2} - t) + (8t - 4) &=  \\\\\nt(2t - 1) + 4(2t - 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (2t - 1)(t + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n$-5(2t - 1)(t + 4)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" :question/math-academy-id "e-15089" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20$." :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5$. So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n$5(3x^{2} - 8x + 4)$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12$.\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n-2-6= -8 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x$, and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 8x + 4 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 2x - 6x + 4 &=  \\\\\n3x^{2} - 6x - 2x + 4 &=  \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 6x) - (2x - 4) &=  \\\\\n3x(x - 2) - 2(x - 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (3x - 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n$5(3x - 2)(x - 2)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3597fbc-489c-57d8-af2e-580b42c1d248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200934} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb0d2cc-c7e8-5a98-88d7-9188c92b2649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x + 4)(2x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200935} {:answer/id #uuid "f41945ac-298a-5364-8aa9-3d1a7559e17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)(2x + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200936} {:answer/id #uuid "2d02ffdb-4155-5fd4-a868-8887ba33823d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)(4x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200937} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab963c1-69aa-5f20-a9bf-1a976dcf7676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 4)(3x + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200938}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e52abdf3-c0dc-5ef1-894f-dee194666a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200933}] :question/id #uuid "6a1a0c7d-b300-5f97-894e-7b4ae6893dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-225002" :question/problem "Factor $6x^{2} + 26x + 8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "90c7b183-e533-502b-8176-8388d1565702" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200939} {:answer-field/id #uuid "17c3c1df-a7e2-5642-ad06-78f1c0578359" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200940} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7af37f00-7de9-5a1f-b9ee-080fd67e4675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200941}] :question/id #uuid "98b7e6f2-e40d-535e-a12b-3e5bd59b4957" :question/math-academy-id "q-225073" :question/problem "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.$8y^{2} - 36y + 40 =$\n{{field-1}} $($\n{{field-2}} $y - 5)(y -$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$"}]}
